        <h2>Is Kottu Vegetarian?</h2>

        <p>Yes, with some caveats. Egg kottu with vegetables and no meat is genuinely vegetarian if you are not strict about eggs. Cheese kottu without meat is also vegetarian. The curry sauce used to flavour the kottu may contain chicken or beef stock — worth asking if you are strictly vegetarian. Paneer kottu is available at many restaurants as a fully vegetarian protein option.</p>

        <p>Pure vegetarian kottu is common in the north and east of Sri Lanka, where Tamil vegetarian restaurants are widespread. In these areas, you can get a fully vegan kottu with no egg or dairy.</p>

        <h2>Tips for Ordering Kottu</h2>

        <p><strong>Specify your protein first:</strong> "Chicken kottu, one" is the standard ordering format. The cook will assume a medium spice level.</p>

        <p><strong>Ask about spice:</strong> Default kottu is reasonably hot. If you want it milder, say "not spicy" (karavadda næhæ). If you want it hotter — rare but possible — ask for extra chilli.</p>

        <p><strong>The side curry matters:</strong> The small cup of curry sauce served on the side is not decoration — it's an integral part of the dish. Pour it over the kottu or dip pieces into it.</p>

        <p><strong>Eat it hot:</strong> Kottu is at its best in the first five minutes. It cools and tightens as it sits; eat immediately, standing at the counter if necessary.</p>

        <p><strong>Order a Lion lager:</strong> The combination of cold Lion beer and a plate of kottu at 9:30pm is one of the canonical Sri Lankan travel experiences.</p>

